# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L269
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L285-L293
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L382-L397


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The liquidity amount to deposit from the ILOPool on launch is wrongly calculated. Assumes that the uniswap pool price is static, for projects with already circulating supply this is not the case. 

When the deposit amount of the payment token (the one given by investors) is less than the amount in the ILOPool, this leftover will be stuck in the contract (loss of funds)

## Proof of Concept
The [launch function](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L269) deposits liquidity into the uniswap pool. For this it calculates the amount of token0 and token1 to deposit using [_saleAmountNeeded](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L382). This function assumes that the price is static, which is a wrong assumption for projects that have already circulating supply between investors. 

```solidity
    function _saleAmountNeeded(uint256 raiseAmount) internal view returns (
        uint256 saleAmountNeeded,
        uint128 liquidity
    ) {
        if (raiseAmount == 0) return (0, 0);

        if (_cachedPoolKey.token0 == SALE_TOKEN) {
            // liquidity1 raised
            // SQRT_RATIO_X96 IS THE CURRENT PRICE. STATIC AND NOT FETCHED FROM POOL
            liquidity = LiquidityAmounts.getLiquidityForAmount1(SQRT_RATIO_LOWER_X96, SQRT_RATIO_X96, raiseAmount);
            saleAmountNeeded = SqrtPriceMathPartial.getAmount0Delta(SQRT_RATIO_X96, SQRT_RATIO_UPPER_X96, liquidity, true);
        } else {
            // liquidity0 raised
            // SQRT_RATIO_X96 IS THE CURRENT PRICE. STATIC AND NOT FETCHED FROM POOL
            liquidity = LiquidityAmounts.getLiquidityForAmount0(SQRT_RATIO_X96, SQRT_RATIO_UPPER_X96, raiseAmount);
            saleAmountNeeded = SqrtPriceMathPartial.getAmount1Delta(SQRT_RATIO_LOWER_X96, SQRT_RATIO_X96, liquidity, true);
        }
    }
```

THe function _saleAmountNeeded calculates the sale token amount needed on the pool given the raise amount with the following [formulas](https://uniswapv3book.com/milestone_1/calculating-liquidity.html#token-amounts-calculation-again). This function uses the uniswap library [SqrtPriceMathPartial.getAmount0Delta](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/libraries/SqrtPriceMathPartial.sol#L12-L20C14) and [getAmount1Delta](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/libraries/SqrtPriceMathPartial.sol#L42-L49). These should calculate the amount of token0 and token1 respectively given two prices. 

The problem arises when the price moves and the amount of token returned is wrong. Example:

- Project and ILOPool are deployed and investors already deposited tokens to buy.
- The token0 is the project's token and token1 is the liquidity token given by investors. 
- The price of the pool moves to the right, thus for the position chosen by the project owners between ticks a and b now requires more of token0 (sale token) and less of token1 (raise token).
- The ILOPool contains X amount of token1 (raise token) and since the liquidity position now requires less of this token, two scenarios can happen:
  - The ILOPool contains enough token0 (sale token) to deposit liquidity, thus the  leftovers of token1 are stuck in the contract.
  - The ILOPool doesn't contains enough of token0 to deposit, thus the launch always fails because the ILOPool doesn't transfer enough of token0.
  - [amounts asked](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/base/LiquidityManagement.sol#L20-L29).
  - [actual transfer](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/base/PeripheryPayments.sol#L31-L38)

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
When calculating the token amounts to transfer, don't rely on the stored initial price for projects with active pools.

The amount of tokens transferred by the investors should be refundable after launch. To avoid leftovers on their side.





## Assessed type

Math

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L269
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L285-L293
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L295-L303
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/base/LiquidityManagement.sol#L41-L57


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
When the ILOPool launches on an active uniswap pool (pool with liquidity), it will allow an attacker to frontrun the liquidity minting and benefit from the price change or making the launch fail completely.

## Proof of Concept

Given the [documentation](https://docs.uniswap.org/contracts/v3/guides/providing-liquidity/mint-a-position#calling-mint) of uniswap about minting new positions. The contract that calls "mint" on a pool must verify that the amounts that the contract wants to send matches the amount that the uniswap pool asks (the contract should also define some slippage to avoid reverts for incorrect amounts).

When [calculating](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L285-L293) the amount of token0 and token1 to send, the [amount0Min or amount1Min](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L280-L281) can be equal to 0. This contract verifies that the amounts sent [matches](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/base/LiquidityManagement.sol#L56) the minimum amount asked. 

```solidity
    function addLiquidity(AddLiquidityParams memory params)
        internal
        returns (
            uint256 amount0,
            uint256 amount1
        )
    {
        (amount0, amount1) = params.pool.mint(
            address(this),
            TICK_LOWER,
            TICK_UPPER,
            params.liquidity,
            ""
        );

        require(amount0 >= params.amount0Min && amount1 >= params.amount1Min, 'Price slippage check');
    }
```

We check for the minimum sent of both amounts. The problem is that we never [initialize the minimum sold token amount](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L278-L293) to send. 

```solidity
    function launch() external override OnlyManager() {
       ...
            uint256 amount0;
            uint256 amount1;
            uint256 amount0Min;
            uint256 amount1Min;
            address token0Addr = _cachedPoolKey.token0;

            if (token0Addr == RAISE_TOKEN) { 
                // HERE  amount1Min is NOT initialized
                amount0 = totalRaised;
                amount0Min = totalRaised;
                (amount1, liquidity) = _saleAmountNeeded(totalRaised);
            } else {
                // HERE  amount0Min is NOT initialized
                (amount0, liquidity) = _saleAmountNeeded(totalRaised);
                amount1 = totalRaised;
                amount1Min = totalRaised;
            }

            // WE ADD LIQUIDITY BUT DONT VALIDATE amount0Min or amount1Min since they are NOT INITIALIZED (equal to 0)
            (amount0, amount1) = addLiquidity(AddLiquidityParams({
                pool: IUniswapV3Pool(uniV3PoolAddress),
                liquidity: liquidity,
                amount0Desired: amount0,
                amount1Desired: amount1,
                amount0Min: amount0Min,
                amount1Min: amount1Min
            }));

            ...
    }
```

Thus, a frontrunner can benefit from this amount not being checked by buying or selling. Example:

- ILOPool launches, it wants to send X amount of raise token and Y amount of sold token.

- ILOPool sends the transaction, [checking](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/base/LiquidityManagement.sol#L56) that at least X amount of raise token is sent to the pool. But it [doesn't check(https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/main/src/ILOPool.sol#L280-L293) for the Y amount of sold token, so this can be a very small amount, close to 0. This is because the sold token minimum amount is not initialized.

- A frontrunner sees the tx on the mempool, the frontrunner can manipulate how much of the sold token is deposited from the smart contract. This can be done by buying or selling tokens on the pool and moving its price. This can create two scenarios:
   - The launch fails because the price increases too much and the ILOPool doesn't have enough of the raise token to deposit
   - The frontrunner bough a lot of sold tokens. The ILOPool deposits a very small amount of sold tokens. Then the attacker can sell for a profit, since the ILOPool deposited a lot of the raise token and very little of the sold token (price went up).

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Initialize the minimum amount to send of both tokens and add some slippage calculation in case of price differences during the deposit. This will avoid the contract to revert during the deposit.







## Assessed type

Uniswap

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/cb72b1e9053c02a58d874ff376359a83dc3f0742/src/base/Multicall.sol#L11-L27


# Vulnerability details


## Proof of Concept

Take a look at https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/cb72b1e9053c02a58d874ff376359a83dc3f0742/src/base/Multicall.sol#L11-L27

```solidity
    function multicall(bytes[] calldata data) public payable override returns (bytes[] memory results) {
        results = new bytes[](data.length);
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
            (bool success, bytes memory result) = address(this).delegatecall(data[i]);

            if (!success) {
                // Next 5 lines from https://ethereum.stackexchange.com/a/83577
                if (result.length < 68) revert();
                assembly {
                    result := add(result, 0x04)
                }
                revert(abi.decode(result, (string)));
            }

            results[i] = result;
        }
    }
```

This function enables calling multiple methods in a single call to the contract, now this function is marked as payable which would mean that some of the methods that would be batched would need ether value attached to it.

Problem however is that this is being processed via the `delegatecalling` opcode, from the [solidity docs](https://docs.soliditylang.org/en/latest/introduction-to-smart-contracts.html#delegatecall-and-libraries), we can see that in the case where `delegatecall` is used which is identical to a normal message `call` apart from the fact that the code at the target address is executed in the context (i.e. at the address) of the calling contract and `msg.sender` and` msg.value` do not change their values. So since the msg.values don't change this would mean that batching multiple payable methods via the context of the `multicall` function would never work, cause the execution would attempt to reuse the same `msg.value` for all iterations.

## Impact
Important functionality of protocol is broken, considering the batched payable multicall operations would not work.
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Maybe use `.call` instead, specify the targetaddress and also the data[i].value as the ether value to be attached to the execution.



## Assessed type

ETH-Transfer

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-06-vultisig/blob/cb72b1e9053c02a58d874ff376359a83dc3f0742/src/ILOManager.sol#L63
